{
 "regions": [
  {
   "id": "r2",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "Müller’s History of Ancient Sanskrit Literature, a tradition is",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "mentioned which gives the name Bhartṛihari to the author of",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "the Mahâbhâshya.[*] There must, I think, be some mistake here.",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r3",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "Little more of Bhartṛihari’s personal history is known than",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "has been alluded to in the previous pages. His theosophy, as",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "has been pointed out, corresponds notably with that of the Ve-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 4,
     "text": "dântins. In religion proper, he was a unitarian as respects S'iva",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 5,
     "text": "and Vishṇu.[†] He, however, acknowledges in so many words,",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 6,
     "text": "what is clear enough from various parts of his poems, that his",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 7,
     "text": "special predilections are towards the former Deity.[‡] In the first",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 8,
     "text": "Stanza of the S'ṛingâras'ataka he mentions together Brahmâ,",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 9,
     "text": "Vishṇu and S'iva—who seem to have been already in his time",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 10,
     "text": "associated together into the Hindu Trinity.[§] Banâras is once",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 11,
     "text": "mentioned with reverence, as also, and more frequently, the",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 12,
     "text": "",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 13,
     "text": "Ganges.[¶] The stories of the temptations of Parâs'ara and",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 14,
     "text": "",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 15,
     "text": "Vis'vâmitra are also referred to.[‖] The mention of Vishṇu’s Ten",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 16,
     "text": "Incarnations has been already touched upon.",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r4",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "One great question still remains—that, namely, suggested by",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "some of the Stanzas in these S'atakas being found to occur in other",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "extant works. We need take no particular notice of those which",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 4,
     "text": "",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 5,
     "text": "occur in the Panchatantra ; nor those in the works on Rhetoric",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 6,
     "text": "such as the Das'arûpaka or the Kâvya-Prakâs'a ; nor yet of the",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 7,
     "text": "one in the Mahânâṭaka ; nor even of those quoted in the Bhojapra-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 8,
     "text": "",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 9,
     "text": "bandha ; nor again of that well known Stanza of the S'âkuntala",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 10,
     "text": "commencing अनाघ्रातं पुष्पम् which, according to Weber’s Various",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
